\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


Write $\mathbb D=\{z\in\mathbb C:|z|<1\}$ and $\mathbb T=\partial\mathbb D$. Let $p(z)=\sum_{k=0}^na_kz^k$ be a nonconstant complex polynomial of degree $n\ge1$ whose zeros all lie on $\mathbb T$. Put
\[A(p)=\max_{0\le k\le n}|a_k|,\qquad M(p)=\max_{|z|=1}|p(z)|.
\]
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is $M(p)\ge2A(p)$ for every such polynomial? \href{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Does a polynomial with all zeros on the unit circle have maximum modulus there at least twice its largest coefficient?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2304002 (AMR-022-4002). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] Walter K. Hayman and Edward F. Lingham, \emph{Research Problems in Function Theory}, arXiv:1809.07200, 2018. Problem 4.2, its complete Update 4.2, and the relevant chapter notation. \url{https://arxiv.org/pdf/1809.07200}.
\end{description}
